{
  "content": "The task-list argument is itself a JSON-encoded string, not a JSON array value. Encode it as valid JSON, escaping quotes, backslashes, and other required characters; do not include comments. In each task’s dependencies array, reference prerequisite task names exactly, not task IDs. Use an empty array when there are no prerequisites.",
  "applicability": "When calling the task-splitting operation, especially when task descriptions contain JSON-special characters or tasks depend on one another.",
  "actions": [
    "Construct the task list as valid JSON, then serialize that JSON as the string value for the task-list argument.",
    "Escape characters according to JSON string rules and omit comments.",
    "Represent each dependency by the exact name of its prerequisite task."
  ],
  "verification": "Readbacks showed dependencies supplied as task names were stored and displayed as references to the corresponding task IDs. Confirm the resulting task details show the intended prerequisite links; this verifies task-manager effects, not the correctness of the planned or implemented software."
}

{
  "content": "The execution operation returns task guidance and changes the task's recorded status from pending to in progress; it does not implement the requested work. Detailed task lookup provides task information but does not establish that implementation or verification has occurred.",
  "applicability": "When using task-management tools, interpret execution responses as guidance and workflow state, not as evidence that code or other requested artifacts were produced.",
  "actions": [
    "Read the returned guidance and use it to carry out the requested work separately.",
    "Inspect the task details when more context or verification criteria are needed.",
    "Check the actual artifact and perform relevant tests before claiming implementation is complete."
  ],
  "verification": "Observed task status changed to in progress after execution guidance was retrieved, while the checked artifact remained absent. These checks establish the tools' observed effects only; they do not establish general software correctness."
}
